  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 358 AM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nCurrent upper-level water vapor imagery showing troughing over\nNew Brunswick, Canada departing northeast, while broad \ntroughing enters the PacNorthwest. Saddled in between these two \nfeatures is a riding pattern that extends from the \nIntermountain-West over to the Great Lakes region. Below this \nridging pattern, a shortwave was located over the MN and Dakotas\nborder that will progress to the east southeast today. Mid-\nlevel warm advection combined with an intensifying 40-50kt low \nlevel jet will aid in additional moisture transport into the \nnorthern plains. This has resulted in rainfall becoming more \nwidespread since 1 AM this morning across much of western \nMinnesota and will continue east over the next several hours. A \nfew rumbles of thunder could be common, especially across \nwestern MN where an axis of mixed layer instability between \n500-1000 J/k exists. Much of this rainfall should be captured \nduring the first half of today. Although most areas can expect \nthe rainfall to wrap up by 10 AM this morning, western Wisconsin\nwill lag closer to noon before most rain has passed on. Most \nareas have a favorable shot at picking up 0.25\" of rainfall with\nlocalized areas receiving closer to 0.75\". Once the rain \nclears, sky coverage will vary depending on location across \nMN/WI today. For those south and west of I-94 have a stronger \nshot at seeing partly cloudy skies whereas elsewhere can expect \nbroken sunshine by this afternoon. High temperatures this \nafternoon will also reflect where sunshine is more prevalent, \ni.e southwest MN reaching the mid to upper 80s, eastern MN near \n80, western WI, mid to low 70s. \n\nAfter today, the weather pattern becomes more consistent with\nzonal flow aloft, warmer temperatures, and daily rainfall \nchances. On Sunday, another subtle wave moves across central MN \nSunday morning featuring a broad surface low that will bring \nanother chance for showers and potentially a strong storm Sunday\nafternoon. Latest forecast guidance favor more of central MN \ntrack this time but given some inconsistencies on storm \ncoverage, was reluctant to go astray from current NBM PoPs. Key \ntakeaway here is that most areas are better suited to remain dry\nversus seeing rain. As for temps, Sunday and Monday will feel \nthe most humid with dew points in the low 70s and highs in the \nupper-80s across southern MN. As the week continues, dew points \nwill improve slightly but highs will stay in the 80s with lows \nin 60s.",
